    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    {#- F5 reveal-boot. F5. The reveal system's HIDDEN state must exist before the first
           paint, and this is the only thing that can put it there.

           It used to be gated on `body.js-loaded`, which init.js adds on
           DOMContentLoaded -- i.e. AFTER the browser has already painted the
           page with every reveal element fully opaque. The 1000ms transition
           was declared in the same rule, so the class landing animated the
           content from visible to invisible: measured on /locations/, below-fold
           elements read opacity 1 at 37ms and 0 at 288ms. Content appeared,
           faded OUT over about a second, and faded back in on scroll. A reveal
           that has to un-reveal first is not a reveal.

           A synchronous inline script is the fix rather than a preference: it
           runs during head parsing, before the body exists, so `.js` is on the
           root element for the very first style resolution. Deferred or external
           JS cannot be, by definition.

           The transitions are deliberately NOT enabled here -- `.js .reveal`
           carries opacity and transform only. init.js adds `.reveal-ready` after
           the first paint has committed the hidden state, so the load-in
           animates FROM hidden instead of animating TO it.
        CSP: this script is inline BY NECESSITY (parse-time, pre-first-paint) and is
        allowlisted by sha256 hash in netlify.toml. The body below must stay EXACTLY
        one line — any change (even whitespace) breaks the hash and the CSP silently
        kills the script on Netlify only. scripts/csp-inline-hash.test.mjs recomputes
        the hash from the built output and fails on drift, either side. -#}
    <script data-ssc="reveal-boot">document.documentElement.classList.add('js')</script>
